WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.460
Did Queen Elizabeth II hate-watch The&nbsp;
Crown?! Apparently she was a regular viewer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.960
even if she detested how one character&nbsp;
was portrayed. And no, it wasn't her!

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:14.580
In addition to receiving numerous awards&nbsp;
for its cast, costumes, and effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:19.680
Netflix's The Crown has earned its share&nbsp;
of criticisms, particularly regarding its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.180
relationship to real-life history. The series&nbsp;
focuses on the lives of the royal family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:30.180
starting when Queen Elizabeth II unexpectedly&nbsp;
becomes Queen at the young age of 25.

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:33.669
"But for better or worse, the&nbsp;
crown has landed on my head."

00:00:33.669 --> 00:00:38.700
While its individual storylines are derived from&nbsp;
actual events, much of the show is embellished,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:43.740
consisting of fictionalized accounts of&nbsp;
conversations and interactions that may or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:49.020
may not have actually happened. At one point, the&nbsp;
United Kingdom's culture secretary, Oliver Dowden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:53.400
said that The Crown should add a fiction&nbsp;
disclaimer. Dowden told The Mail on Sunday,

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.040
"I fear a generation of viewers who did not live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.440
through these events may&nbsp;
mistake fiction for fact."

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.400
While both Netflix and The Crown&nbsp;
creator Peter Morgan have stated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.240
they believe audiences to be smart enough&nbsp;
to figure out that the show is, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.520
fiction, this sentiment is not shared by&nbsp;
all relevant parties. Helena Bonham Carter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:16.440
who portrays Princess Margaret in Seasons 3&nbsp;
and 4, told the official podcast for the show:

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:20.520
"['The Crown'] is dramatized. I do&nbsp;
feel very strongly because I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:26.040
we have a moral responsibility to say, 'Hang&nbsp;
on guys, this is not ... it's not a drama-doc,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.980
we're making a drama.' So there&nbsp;
are two different entities."

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:33.360
While many royals have made it clear&nbsp;
they aren't fans of Netflix's The Crown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:39.120
the late Queen Elizabeth II expressed that she was&nbsp;
most upset about how Prince Philip was portrayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:44.580
as a callous and insensitive father to Prince&nbsp;
Charles. A senior royal source told Express:

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:48.660
"She was particularly annoyed at a&nbsp;
scene in which Philip has no sympathy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:52.320
for a plainly upset Charles while he&nbsp;
is flying him home from Scotland."

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:58.080
In Season 2, Episode 9, Prince Philip flies a&nbsp;
clearly upset Prince Charles home from Gordonstoun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:03.180
after Charles attempts, and fails, to participate&nbsp;
in a school challenge. As Philip tries to tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:07.800
Charles how he did during the challenge is of no&nbsp;
consequence to him, Charles looks frightened and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:12.911
anxious as they fly through turbulence. Philips&nbsp;
continues with his pep talk, telling Charles,

00:02:12.911 --> 00:02:14.196
"It's just air."

00:02:14.196 --> 00:02:16.800
Phillip then tells his son&nbsp;
that he's weak and orders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.040
him to the back of the plane. The&nbsp;
senior royal source told Express:

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.960
"That simply did not happen."

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:26.340
Despite her distaste for the way The Crown&nbsp;
depicts Prince Philip's relationship with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:30.480
a young Prince Charles, it's since been&nbsp;
publicly revealed that Queen Elizabeth II,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.840
did, in fact, regularly&nbsp;
watch The Crown on Netflix.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:38.820
A report by Entertainment Weekly recounts how,&nbsp;
during a broadcast of the Queen's funeral,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:44.880
leading royal authority George Jobson happened&nbsp;
to mention that Queen Elizabeth II once made a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:48.420
habit of watching The Crown on Sundays.&nbsp;
He even brought up the fact that Prince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:52.500
Philip would sometimes discourage his wife&nbsp;
from tuning in, knowing that it would make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:57.180
her upset. This latter detail suggests that the&nbsp;
relationship between Prince Philip and Prince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.860
Charles might not have been the Queen's only&nbsp;
gripe with the show, but rather one of many.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:06.600
Given that it mines their real lives for drama,&nbsp;
it's no surprise that members of the royal family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:11.820
may have taken issue with some of the contents of&nbsp;
The Crown. Of course, while series creator Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:15.720
Morgan has stated he plans to make at least&nbsp;
six seasons, whether or not Queen Elizabeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:20.957
II's passing affects the show's future could&nbsp;
remain a point of interest moving forward.

